-- title:
--   Universal worst-case slope-three lower bound
-- statement:
--   For the discrete-phase, ancilla-free one-qubit Clifford+T gate set generated by $H,S,T,\omega I$, there is an absolute real constant $K$ such that
--
--   $$
--   \forall\varepsilon>0\ \exists U\in SU(2)\ \forall c,\qquad
--   \|U_c-U\|_{\mathrm{op}}\le\varepsilon\ \Longrightarrow\ T(c)\ge K+3\log_2(1/\varepsilon).
--   $$
--
--   The quantifier over $c$ ranges over all finite chronological gate words; $T(c)$ counts their T instructions, and $U_c$ is their exact matrix product. No phase is removed when measuring the spectral error. The hard operator may depend on $\varepsilon$, while $K$ is uniform. No positivity is required of $K$. This is the counting-based worst-case consequence of section 9.
-- source:
--   Efficient Clifford+T approximation of single-qubit operators, arXiv:1212.6253v2, PDF p. 13, §9, equation (36): universal worst-case slope-3 lower bound

